-\abstract{Streaming large language model (LLM) services can expose response-length metadata even when payloads are encrypted. We evaluate a fixed T5 reconstructor using validated application-layer byte-length traces from 300 aligned prompts for each of seven open-weight models. Reconstruction of 2100 archived responses under a 512-token victim budget yields mean term-frequency cosine scores ($\phi$) of 0.482--0.535 for five models. TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini retain malformed archived word boundaries and are analyzed as diagnostics. Separate generation of 300 responses per model with verified full-sequence detokenization yields mean scores of 0.435 and 0.504; these new cohorts are not pooled into the archived comparison. In 96-token pilots, replacing the trace by a constant reduces lexical scores for both tested models, while shuffling is significant only for Qwen after multiplicity correction; cross-prompt contrasts are inconclusive. Five framing transformations reduce frozen-reconstructor mean scores by 36.7--88.7\%. A 3300-reconstruction study with five decoder seeds and a prompt-disjoint maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) length inverse recovers much of the score lost under bucketing and batching. This study decodes the first heuristic segment against the full response; the primary experiment decodes all segments. Input and output limits of 80 T5 tokens make segmentation and retained encoder coverage part of each comparison. The results establish reproducible lexical behavior of this instrumented pipeline, without measuring semantic disclosure, intrinsic model vulnerability, or passive recovery from encrypted transport.}
+\abstract{Streaming large language model (LLM) services can expose response-length metadata even when payloads are encrypted. We evaluate a fixed T5 reconstructor through 6600 reconstructions from validated application-layer length traces. For 300 aligned prompts per model, five archived-reference cohorts yield mean term-frequency cosine scores ($\phi$) of 0.482--0.535; two additional archived cohorts have malformed word boundaries. Newly generated TinyLlama and Phi-3.5-mini responses with verified detokenization yield means of 0.435 and 0.504. Legacy rendering of their saved token IDs reproduces all 600 corresponding archived references and length vectors. Changing both the trace and scoring reference increases mean $\phi$ by 0.356 and 0.383 under matched reconstruction settings. In two-model pilots, three of six partial-ablation contrasts remain significant after Holm correction; neither cross-prompt contrast is conclusive. Five framing transformations reduce frozen-reconstructor mean scores by 36.7--88.7\%. A five-seed study with a maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) length inverse recovers much of the loss under bucketing and batching. Reconstruction scopes and retained encoder coverage differ across these experiments. The findings quantify lexical behavior of an instrumented pipeline and demonstrate the importance of validating both observations and reference rendering. They do not measure semantic disclosure or demonstrate passive extraction from encrypted transport.}
 
